  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🥂 Catering Business Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for a catering company, from home-cooked buffets to wedding feasts."
    },
    "intro": "Good catering names sound trustworthy enough for a wedding and simple enough to read on a van: a family name (<em>Granger &amp; Family Caterers</em>), a warm pun (<em>Pinch of Salt Catering</em>) or a short modern word (<em>Cloche</em>). This **catering business name generator** gives you names in seven styles, from punny to elegant, including ones built from your town or your own name.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good catering names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator:</p><ul><li><strong>Pinch of Salt Catering</strong>: a cooking phrase everyone knows, with a light touch.</li><li><strong>Hog Heaven Roasts</strong>: says exactly what you serve, and makes people smile.</li><li><strong>Simply Platters</strong>: short, plain and right for grazing boards and buffets.</li><li><strong>Rosemary Picnics</strong>: a herb and an occasion, for a softer, outdoor brand.</li><li><strong>Granger &amp; Family Caterers</strong>: a family name reads as a firm with a reputation.</li><li><strong>Cloche</strong>: one modern word: the silver dome lifted off a plate at the table.</li><li><strong>Laurel &amp; Sage Wedding Catering</strong>: two herbs and the word clients search for.</li><li><strong>Champagne &amp; Canapés</strong>: says weddings and drinks receptions before anyone reads the menu.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "What makes a catering name work",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Trust first.</strong> Clients are handing you their wedding or their company party. A family name, your own name or a calm, classic word does more than a joke for formal events.</li><li><strong>Say the occasion if you specialise.</strong> People search for \"wedding catering\", \"hog roast\" or \"afternoon tea\", so a name that includes it is easier to find.</li><li><strong>Leave room to grow.</strong> <em>Sarah's Sunday Roasts</em> is lovely until you start doing canapés. A wider word like <em>Kitchen</em>, <em>Catering Co.</em> or <em>Events</em> gives you room.</li><li><strong>Canapé, cloche, soirée:</strong> French words suit the luxury end of the market, because the vocabulary of formal dining came from France. Make sure clients can spell them when they search.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Check it's free before you print the sign",
